delegates arrived in the capital bamako  for the talks organized by mali's  government dominated by military leaders  the four-day forum is aiming to pave the  way for the return of civilian role  following last year's call  mali's transitional government expects  delegates to make concrete proposals for  ending the political crisis  and to prevent further sanctions from  the regional organization a course  this is our country they can't wish for  the good of our country more than we  do mali has a long history of national  consultations to discuss problems and  recommend solutions  but this time the opposition aren't  attending insisting it was a waste of  time they are demanding a swift return  to democratic rule  but some delegates disagree  it is not possible to hold elections  the people of mali have suffered  political instability since gaining  independence from france 61 years ago  in august last year the military toppled  president ibrahim buba kaketa after  weeks of street protests against  corruption and insecurity  another coup in may  disrupted the military's pledge for  elections in february  no group has claimed responsibility for  wednesday's attack in nara town on the  border with mauritania  it's a potential victim in four sau  we need to dialogue with them exchange  with them to disarm their hearts and  minds as long as we don't do that as  soon as they have an occasion they will  attack  attacks by and groups in the restive  sahel desert region have killed  thousands and displaced millions despite  years of efforts by international forces  to defeat them  it's feared further attacks will cause  yet more instability  fidelis  al jazeera abuja
 

